.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SHUTDOWN 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm shutdown
.Nd bring the system down at a stated time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl rhHPkc
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Ar time
.Op Ar message
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
waits out a delay, says on the console what is about to happen, and then asks
.Xr init 8
for the runlevel that does it.
The work itself is
.Xr init 8 Ns 's :
this program only decides when, and what to tell whoever is watching.
.Pp
It stays in the foreground while it waits, so a delayed shutdown is a process
that can be seen with
.Xr ps 1
and interrupted.
Ctrl-C cancels it; so does
.Nm Fl c
from another terminal, which finds it through
.Pa /run/shutdown.pid .
.Pp
.Ar time
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "+minutes" -offset indent
.It Cm now
immediately.
.It Cm + Ns Ar minutes
that many minutes from now.
.It Ar hh : Ns Ar mm
at that time of day.
A time already past today is taken as tomorrow.
.El
.Pp
.Ar message
is appended to each announcement, and is the place to say why.
.Pp
Announcements go to
.Pa /dev/console .
System V wrote to every logged-in terminal, which it found in utmp; nothing on
a Sowa system writes utmp, so the console is the one place a warning can be
counted on to appear.
They are made at an hour, half an hour, fifteen, ten, five, two and one minutes
remaining, then at thirty and ten seconds, then every second from five down to
one, and once more as the request goes out.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-t seconds"
.It Fl r
Reboot: runlevel 6.
.It Fl h
Power the system off: runlevel 0.
.It Fl P
The same as
.Fl h .
.It Fl H
Halt without powering off, which leaves the machine stopped with the power on.
.It Fl k
Announce the shutdown but do not perform it.
.It Fl c
Cancel a shutdown that is waiting, and announce that it was cancelled.
.It Fl t Ar seconds
How long a process gets between the
.Dv SIGTERM
that asks it to leave the runlevel and the
.Dv SIGKILL
that makes it.
The default is 3.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl r ,
.Fl h ,
.Fl H
or
.Fl P
the system goes to single user mode, which is what System V's
.Nm
has always done with a bare time argument.
.Pp
.Fl a , Fl f , Fl F , Fl n
and
.Fl q
are accepted and ignored: there is no fsck here to force or skip, and nothing
writes wtmp.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/run/shutdown.pid" -compact
.It Pa /run/shutdown.pid
the waiting
.Nm ,
so that
.Fl c
can find it.
.It Pa /run/initctl
the FIFO the runlevel request is written to.
.It Pa /dev/console
where announcements go.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A cancelled shutdown exits 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Reboot in five minutes, saying why:
.Pp
.Dl shutdown -r +5 \(dqkernel upgrade\(dq
.Pp
Change your mind:
.Pp
.Dl shutdown -c
.Pp
Power off at half past two in the morning:
.Pp
.Dl shutdown -h 02:30
.Pp
Rehearse the announcements without taking anything down:
.Pp
.Dl shutdown -k +1 \(dqjust checking\(dq
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr halt 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr telinit 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Only root can shut the system down.
.Pp
A
.Nm
that is killed with
.Dv SIGKILL
rather than cancelled leaves
.Pa /run/shutdown.pid
behind.
.Pa /run
is a tmpfs, so the next boot has none.
